WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.570
Choose a point at x equals a and a second
point h units away.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.586
The line through them is a secant line.

3
00:00:08.586 --> 00:00:12.894
Its slope divides the change in function value
by the change in input.

4
00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:18.791
That number describes average change across
the interval, not yet what happens at one

5
00:00:18.791 --> 00:00:19.633
exact point.

6
00:00:19.754 --> 00:00:23.345
Now shrink h without setting it equal to zero.

7
00:00:23.345 --> 00:00:29.855
As the second point approaches the first, the
secant lines approach a limiting position: the

8
00:00:29.855 --> 00:00:30.723
tangent line.

9
00:00:30.723 --> 00:00:37.040
If their slopes approach one finite value,
that value is the derivative f prime of a.

10
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.918
The limit turns nearby average change into
instantaneous change.

11
00:00:42.026 --> 00:00:47.293
A positive derivative means the function is
increasing locally; a negative derivative

12
00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:52.560
means it is decreasing; and a zero derivative
gives a horizontal tangent.

13
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.731
Close to the point, the tangent line can
approximate the curve.

14
00:00:56.731 --> 00:01:02.179
The derivative is therefore both a geometric
slope and a practical rate of change.
